./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:465:## [locked-test] locked-test-europe-not-untouched — finder medium / claim-unsupported → VERDICT confirmed / medium
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:466:**About 13% of the 'untouched' Europe test sites were training and held-out CV sites in earlier work** (ANALYSIS_PLAN_LOCKED.md; prep_log.md; PROMPT_mshi_v3.md (evidence: scripts/reproduce.py; original repo scripts/task2_sweep.py) plan L3, L7-8; prep_log L157; PROMPT L19; reproduce.py L80-82; repo HEAD a809a97 task2_sweep.py L84, item1_c2_train_fnpp.py L70, item1_c3_train_full_modis.py L99)
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:468:DESCRIPTION: The plan (L3, L7-8), prep_log section 10 (L157) and the PROMPT (L19) say Europe was never used to score any model. That is not true. The original 'Asia' bounding box (25-180E) contained European sites. 30 of the 235 locked-test Europe sites are in the original Asia-box training tables. Those rows were (a) training data for the original models, whose feature set (C8 = 'F') and XGB parameters were carried into the locked candidate set, and (b) held-out validation sites in 5-degree spatial-block CV, both in the original repo and in the audit's own reproduce.py run before the lock. The audit used that CV to argue 'Asia-only selection would have picked Full+MODIS' (prep_log L69). This is literally 'a model trained on Asia(-box) sites scored on European sites' before the lock. The information that leaked is small, because no Europe-specific score was reported, and the locked selection rule is mechanical. But the 'untouched' claim as written is false, and a referee who reads the original repo's box definition will find this.
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:472:AFFECTED: How the locked test is described (D5: 'one clean test scored once on untouched sites'); plan L3/L7-8, prep_log L157, PROMPT L19. The numbers themselves are unchanged.
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:476:VERIFIER: I reproduced every number in this finding on my own, and found one point that makes it stronger. 28 of the test sites contain the identical SRDB record (same row id) that sits in the original Asia-box training tables, so this is the same data, not just nearby sites. The bundle also contradicts itself: prep_log L78/L84 and checkpoint1/site_geography.json already say the 25-180E 'Asia' box held 65 European sites (Estonia 21, Finland 12, Romania 3, all of Russia). Yet plan L3, L7-8, prep_log L157 and PROMPT L19 say Europe was never used to score anything. The original CV code (task2_sweep.py L84 at a809a97 on the v1 table, item1_c2_train_fnpp.py L70, item1_c3_train_full_modis.py L99, and the audit's reproduce.py L81, which ran before the lock) applies no region filter. In k-fold CV every complete row is held out exactly once, so all of these European rows were scored as held-out sites. Those fold scores fed the audit's 'Asia-only selection would have picked Full+MODIS' argument (prep_log L69). The severity should stay medium, not high. No Europe-specific score was ever computed. The locked model trains only on v3 continent==Asia, which excludes these sites. Selection was mechanical. So no reported number changes, but the word 'untouched' is false and must be corrected. 7 of the 30 sites are Russia west of 60E, which the original box counted as Asia. The other 23 are in unambiguously European countries.
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:490:FIX: Keep the pre-registered verdict, but state that the CI is conditional on the fitted model and assumes independent sites. Add post-hoc intervals, labelled as such: a two-sample bootstrap (resample Asia sites and refit OLS-TP; resample Europe sites) and a 5-degree block bootstrap. Phrase the result as 'small positive R2; lower bound not robust to spatial dependence or training-sample uncertainty'.
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:520:AFFECTED: The credibility of the 'pre-registered / locked' claim; reproducibility of the locked numbers (the PROMPT allows ±0.01 because the inputs must be re-downloaded).
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:524:VERIFIER: L61 reads the first token of the .sha256 sidecar and never opens or hashes ANALYSIS_PLAN_LOCKED.md. I confirmed this by running the script. The integrity checks all pass, so nothing was tampered with. The provenance gaps are real: the output JSON has no features hash, package versions, run time or per-site predictions, and no features_v3 parquet is in the bundle. One evidence statement is now out of date in a way that strengthens the finding. The plan's only git commit is b98b2ce, the Phase-0 copy, about 27.5 h AFTER the ~08:25Z 2026-11-01 scoring. a809a97 has no plan file. That commit must not be read as the lock time. Severity stays medium, because it changes how 'pre-registered' can be described (internally time-stamped only). The missing external timestamp was already flagged as [OPEN] in prep_log L156; what is new is that the proposed remedy (commit now) cannot establish precedence. The code defect itself is latent, and PROMPT L104 already requires the fix.
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:552:AFFECTED: None of the reported results. It matters for the rebuild and for any future pre-registered selection.
./docs/review/2026-11-02/handoff_critique.md:637:FIX: Retransform with class-specific smearing, or fit the class model as a log-link GLM (Gamma or quasi-Poisson) for totals. Report class effects on the arithmetic scale. Pre-register the retransformation in the Phase-3 plan, and show both if the choice is contested (measurement noise vs real heterogeneity).
